EDITS = [{'id': 'P52-01', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'so the commitment alone gives a narrower advantage than it may appear to. What does constrain substitution is the log: the entry written at the access event, commitment included, cannot be altered without failing its inclusion proof (Section 4.8).', 'new': 'so the advantage over this rival is narrower than first stated.', 'why': 'his P47 Rival 4 text restored verbatim (audit 5 Oct)'}, {'id': 'P52-02', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'That information cannot be produced from a record that no longer says whose data were accessed. Retaining the link is therefore necessary for compliance with a legal obligation within the meaning of Article 17(3)(b), and an erasure request reaches the payload but not the link, at least for the period Article 9(2) fixes. Article 9 is framed around the health professional access service, and whether every cross-border access event routed through a National Contact Point falls within it is a question of how the access is made; the patient-facing audit-log interface of MyHealth@EU (Chapter 1, Section 1.2) serves the same purpose for those that do not. Beyond the Article 9(2) period the link is kept for the rest of the floor on the strength of the deployment binding, which is illustrative here (Section 4.3), and it is removed at the terminal transition (Section 4.8). In a Secure Processing Environment the position differs, because the ceiling may reach the link (Section 4.6).', 'new': 'That information cannot be produced from a record that no longer says whose data were accessed. For the controller that must provide it, or supply the records it is produced from, retaining the link is therefore necessary for compliance with a legal obligation within the meaning of Article 17(3)(b), and an erasure request reaches the payload but not the link, at least for the period Article 9(2) fixes and once Article 9 applies. The argument has two limits. Article 9 is framed around the health professional access service, so whether it binds the controller of a foreign National Contact Point\'s audit repository, and covers every cross-border access event, depends on how the access is made and how the Member States assign the duty; where it does not, the patient-facing audit-log interface of MyHealth@EU (Chapter 1, Section 1.2) gives the link a purpose but not a legal obligation, and Article 17(3)(b) does not follow. And beyond the Article 9(2) period the link is kept for the rest of the floor on the strength of the deployment binding, which is illustrative here (Section 4.3); for that stretch its retention after an erasure request is exactly as contestable as the floor itself. It is removed at the terminal transition (Section 4.8). In a Secure Processing Environment Article 9 does not apply. There the link is kept after an erasure request until the ceiling only if auditing "all processing operations" under Article 73(1)(e) needs it, which is the question Chapter 7, Section 7.4 leaves to the supervisory authority, and the ceiling may reach it in any case (Section 4.6).', 'why': 'patient-link argument: holder of the obligation, years beyond Art 9, SPE (audit 5 Oct)'}, {'id': 'P52-03', 'file': 'Front_Matter_v1.md', 'old': 'and the link to the patient, which it removes, and with entries', 'new': "and the link to the patient, which it keeps on the cross-border path where the person's access-information right needs it and removes from Secure Processing Environment logs at the ceiling, and with entries", 'why': 'Option A in the Statement of Contribution'}, {'id': 'P52-04', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': "while cross-border records keep it through the floor, because the person's right to learn who accessed their data needs it.", 'new': "while cross-border records keep it, for at least the period the person's right to learn who accessed their data requires and, on the deployment binding, for the rest of the floor.", 'why': '§7.1 softened to match §4.2'}, {'id': 'P52-05', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': "But identifying which records those are reveals the subject's cross-border care history, which is precisely what the erasure was meant to protect.", 'new': "But identifying which records those are would show the auditor the subject's cross-border care history. The controller keeps the link to those records (Chapter 4, Section 4.2); the problem is disclosing it to a third party in order to prove completeness.", 'why': '§7.5 consistent with Option A'}, {'id': 'P52-06', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'This is where succinct arguments become necessary rather than decorative,', 'new': 'This is where succinct arguments may become necessary rather than decorative,', 'why': 'consistent with his §7.5 hedge'}, {'id': 'P52-07', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': '*Corollary 2.3 (sensitivity to what auditing needs).* Partition the payload as $p(a) = p_n(a) \\cup p_r(a)$,', 'new': '*Corollary 2.3 (sensitivity to what auditing needs).* Extend the model with one accessibility variable per part of the payload, which the single-variable model of Section 4.3 cannot express, and partition the payload as $p(a) = p_n(a) \\cup p_r(a)$,', 'why': 'Cor 2.3 stated as an extension (audit)'}, {'id': 'P52-08', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'In that case C1 and C3 collide over $p_n(a)$ for exactly the records that Proposition 3(ii) identifies, and only relocation (Section 4.10, Rival 6) meets both.', 'new': 'In that case C1 and C3 collide over $p_n(a)$, by the argument of Proposition 3, for exactly the records it identifies under case (ii). The collision follows from keeping $p_n(a)$ intact under condition (i) without the logged-removal exception that the subject fields have. Relocation (Section 4.10, Rival 6) is the exit this thesis identifies; logging at dataset granularity, the narrow reading, or anonymising $p_n(a)$ are others.', 'why': 'Cor 2.3 precise (audit)'}, {'id': 'P52-09', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'and Proposition 3 gives the records for which these overlap. ∎', 'new': 'and the argument of Proposition 3 gives the records for which these overlap. ∎', 'why': 'precise'}, {'id': 'P52-10', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'where it fails exactly as Corollary 1.2 does and is answered the same way.', 'new': 'where it fails as Corollary 1.2 does and has the same exits.', 'why': 'precise'}, {'id': 'P52-11', 'file': 'Chapter3_Methodology_v1.md', 'old': 'The simplification is conservative with respect to the propositions, since a mechanism that satisfies the constraints for a whole payload satisfies them for any subset of it, but it means the model says nothing about partial erasure, and a mechanism designed for field-level redaction would need a richer state space than the one used here.', 'new': 'The simplification is conservative with respect to the propositions, since a mechanism that satisfies the constraints for a whole payload satisfies them for any subset of it, but it means the model says nothing about partial erasure. Chapter 4, Corollary 2.3 extends the model with one variable per part of the payload for the one case where that matters, a payload part that auditing needs; a mechanism designed for field-level redaction in general would need that richer state space throughout.', 'why': '§3.7 updated for Cor 2.3'}, {'id': 'P52-12', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'Chapter 3, Section 3.7 records this as a stated abstraction.', 'new': 'Chapter 3, Section 3.7 records this as a stated abstraction, and Corollary 2.3 relaxes it for one case.', 'why': 'consistency'}, {'id': 'P52-13', 'file': 'Chapter1_Introduction_v2.md', 'old': 'Retaining the full plaintext personal-data payload of an audit record for the length of the floor exceeds what accountability requires, since the accountability function of the record can be preserved without continued access to that payload.', 'new': 'Retaining the full plaintext payload of an audit record for the length of the floor exceeds what accountability requires, since the accountability function of the record can be preserved without continued access to that payload; for Secure Processing Environment logs the thesis argues this for the part of the payload auditing does not need (Chapter 4, Corollary 2.3).', 'why': "Prop 2 premise; 'personal-data payload'"}, {'id': 'P52-14', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'The terminal transition, the per-entry nonce, consistency proofs and the accumulator layer are specified but not implemented in the artefact (Chapter 6, Section 6.1).', 'new': 'The terminal transition, the per-entry nonce, consistency proofs and the accumulator layer are specified but not implemented in the artefact (Chapter 6, Section 6.1). For Secure Processing Environment records the result assumes that auditing does not need the payload; Corollary 2.3 states what follows if it needs part of it.', 'why': 'Prop 2 premise in §4.12'}, {'id': 'P52-15', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'Corollary 1.2 stands or falls with this question, and nothing else in the thesis does.', 'new': 'Corollary 1.2 stands or falls with this question, as does the conditional branch of Corollary 2.3; nothing else in the thesis does.', 'why': '§7.4 accurate'}, {'id': 'P52-16', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'Corollary 1.2 stands or falls with it, and nothing else in the thesis does.', 'new': 'Corollary 1.2 stands or falls with it, as does the conditional branch of Corollary 2.3, and nothing else in the thesis does.', 'why': '§6.2 accurate'}, {'id': 'P52-17', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': "The reference implementation of the cross-border gateway keeps such a log. OpenNCP's audit record repository stores every received audit message whole, in a database column for the message content, with the patient identifier in a readable column and a further column for the participant-object query; it refuses to modify a stored message, and it contains no retention, purge or erasure logic, so how long the content stays readable is left to the operator (OpenNCP Project, 2017; Sundhedsdatastyrelsen, 2026). In the 2017 source inspected, the patient-discovery audit carries the patient identifier and the full SOAP security header of the request, though not the demographic query that ITI-55 asks gateways to record; the current schema, deployed with OpenNCP 9.1.0, keeps the column for it.", 'new': 'The reference implementation of the cross-border gateway is built as such a log: whatever an audit message carries stays readable. In the 2017 OpenNCP source, the audit record repository stored every received audit message whole, in a database column for the message content, with the patient identifier in a readable column and a further column for the participant-object query; it refused to modify a stored message, and its audit code had no scheduled retention or purge logic, so how long the content stayed readable was left to the operator (OpenNCP Project, 2017). The schema deployed with OpenNCP 9.1.0 in 2026 keeps the same columns (Sundhedsdatastyrelsen, 2026); whether they are populated in a given deployment was not inspected. In the 2017 source the patient-discovery audit carried the patient identifier and the full SOAP security header of the request, which in the model are subject and accessor fields, though not the demographic query that ITI-55 asks gateways to record. "Verification" there meant that the sending gateway signed each audit message (enveloped XML signature); the repository stored the signature but did not check it, and kept no hash chain or other tamper evidence beyond its refusal to modify.', 'why': "OpenNCP claims tied to what was inspected; 'verification' answered (audit)"}, {'id': 'P52-18', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': "because the patient-discovery transaction puts the patient's identifying query into the audit record by design", 'new': "because the IHE patient-discovery profile requires the patient's identifying query to be recorded in the audit message", 'why': 'consistent with the OpenNCP finding'}, {'id': 'P52-19', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': '\n\nA bounded check in a different formalism narrows that limitation. An optional module poses each proposition to the Z3 SMT solver as a request for a counterexample: a record with any parameter values inside stated bounds (creation month 0–12, floor 1–24 months, response period 1–3 months, request month and ceiling anchor anywhere up to a 64-month horizon, ceiling 1–24 months) that contradicts either direction of Proposition 1, the constructive trajectory of Proposition 2, or either direction of Proposition 3 and its two cases. The solver finds none in any of the nine checks. It does find counterexamples to deliberately wrong versions of Proposition 3 shifted by one month, which shows the checks can fail (Appendix A, A.23 and A.24). The check covers every parameter combination within the bounds rather than a sample, and it shares no code with the checker; it takes the constraint definitions from Section 4.4, so it checks that the propositions follow from the constraints, not that the constraints translate the law, and it is bounded rather than general.', 'new': '', 'why': 'moved below'}, {'id': 'P52-20', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'It is the one place in the demonstration where a parameter was altered for tractability.', 'new': "It is the one place in the checker's demonstration where a parameter was altered for tractability.\n\nA bounded check in a different formalism guards against error in the proofs. An optional module poses the propositions to the Z3 SMT solver as requests for a counterexample: a record with any parameter values inside stated bounds (creation month 0–12, floor 1–120 months, so the cross-border floor is covered, response period 1–3 months, request month and ceiling anchor anywhere up to a 140-month horizon, ceiling 1–120 months). It checks Proposition 1 and the converse Chapter 5 relies on (a record with no deadline inside its window is feasible), the constructive trajectory of Proposition 2 with the accountability state of Semantics II encoded (accessor fields, commitment, invalidation entry, subject link and its removal entry), and both directions of Proposition 3 with its two cases. The solver finds no counterexample in any of the nine checks. It does find counterexamples to deliberately broken versions: the Proposition 2 construction without its invalidation entry, without its commitment, or without the subject-link entry, and Proposition 3 shifted by one month, which shows the checks can fail (Appendix A, A.23 and A.24). The check covers every parameter combination within the bounds rather than a sample and shares no code with the checker. It was written in the same AI-assisted process and takes the constraint definitions from Section 4.4, so it checks that the propositions follow from the constraints as encoded, not that the constraints translate the law, and it is bounded rather than general.", 'why': 'Z3 paragraph moved, accurate, covers F=120 and the full Semantics II encoding'}, {'id': 'P52-21', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'Propositions 3 and 4 are analytic and are not executed here, although Section 5.4 computes the condition of Proposition 3', 'new': 'Proposition 4 is analytic and is not executed here; Section 5.4 computes the condition of Proposition 3 and Section 5.5 checks it', 'why': 'consistency with §5.5'}, {'id': 'P52-22', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': '| `z3_check.py` | 135 |', 'new': '| `z3_check.py` | 153 |', 'why': 'line count'}, {'id': 'P52-23', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'that transition is specified, not implemented.', 'new': 'that transition, the per-entry nonce, consistency proofs and the accumulator layer are specified, not implemented.', 'why': 'comment 6 in Ch7'}, {'id': 'P52-24', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'nor does it implement the per-entry nonce that keeps surviving leaf hashes from confirming guesses', 'new': 'nor does it implement the per-entry nonce that keeps surviving leaf hashes from confirming guesses, or the consistency proofs between successive log roots', 'why': '§6.1 complete'}, {'id': 'P52-25', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'learns what the accountability purpose is argued to require (Section 4.2), while', 'new': 'learns what the accountability purpose is argued to require (Chapter 4, Section 4.2), while', 'why': 'cross-reference in his inserted text'}, {'id': 'P52-26', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'The accumulator is specified but not implemented in the artefact (Section 6.1).', 'new': 'The accumulator is specified but not implemented in the artefact (Chapter 6, Section 6.1).', 'why': 'cross-reference'}, {'id': 'P52-27', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'its leaves omit the nonce accordingly (Section 6.1).', 'new': 'its leaves omit the nonce accordingly (Chapter 6, Section 6.1).', 'why': 'cross-reference'}, {'id': 'P52-28', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'but the terminal transition, which Section 4.8 specifies', 'new': 'but the terminal transition, which Chapter 4, Section 4.8 specifies', 'why': 'cross-reference'}, {'id': 'P52-29', 'file': 'Front_Matter_v1.md', 'old': '- A.22 Console output of the envelope-encryption module', 'new': '- A.22 Console output of the envelope-encryption module\n- A.23 Source listing: `z3_check.py`\n- A.24 Console output of the bounded SMT check', 'why': 'TOC'}, {'id': 'P52-30', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'demonstrates Propositions 1 and 2 executably with an independent cross-check.', 'new': 'checks Propositions 1 and 2 executably, with a separately written cross-check and a bounded SMT check.', 'why': "not 'independent'"}, {'id': 'P52-31', 'file': 'Chapter1_Introduction_v2.md', 'old': 'the independent exhaustive cross-check, a bounded SMT check', 'new': 'the separately written exhaustive cross-check, a bounded SMT check', 'why': "not 'independent'"}, {'id': 'P52-32', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'A deletion ceiling collides with a retention floor exactly when it is no longer than the floor', 'new': 'A deletion ceiling can collide with a retention floor only if it is no longer than the floor', 'why': 'Proposition 3 stated correctly'}, {'id': 'P52-33', 'file': 'Chapter1_Introduction_v2.md', 'old': 'and a personal-data payload bound by a cryptographic commitment,', 'new': 'and a payload bound by a cryptographic commitment,', 'why': 'terminology'}, {'id': 'P52-34', 'file': 'Chapter3_Methodology_v1.md', 'old': 'The personal-data payload comprises the content of the exchanged clinical document.', 'new': 'The payload comprises what the record carries about what was accessed: the demographic query of a cross-border record, the logged activity of a Secure Processing Environment record (Chapter 4, Section 4.3).', 'why': "terminology; stale 'clinical document'"}, {'id': 'P52-35', 'file': 'Chapter3_Methodology_v1.md', 'old': 'the personal-data payload cease to be accessible within the response period.', 'new': 'the payload cease to be accessible within the response period.', 'why': 'terminology'}, {'id': 'P52-36', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'the accessibility of its personal-data payload.', 'new': 'the accessibility of its payload.', 'why': 'terminology'}, {'id': 'P52-37', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The renumbering of these obligations between the 2022 proposal and the adopted text, from Articles 46 and 50 to Articles 68 and 73, is one reason the commentary has treated them apart', 'new': 'The renumbering of these obligations between the 2022 proposal and the adopted text, from Articles 46 and 50 to Articles 68 and 73, may be one reason the commentary has treated them apart', 'why': 'hedge an unsupported causal claim'}, {'id': 'P52-38', 'file': 'Chapter3_Methodology_v1.md', 'old': 'The pivotal move is to make explicit an assumption that the operational status quo leaves implicit.', 'new': 'The pivotal move is to make explicit an assumption that the instruments leave implicit.', 'why': 'no status-quo claim'}]
